    "lawCode": "Amd §420.10, CP L",
    "lawSection": "Criminal Procedure Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6684\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the criminal procedure law, in\nrelation to the payment of certain fines and mandatory surcharges\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nTo clarify that in instances where a court imposes a fine and a\nmandatory surcharge for a violation of the Vehicle and Traffic Law for\nan alcohol drug-related offense, the payment of the fine will take\npriority over payment of the surcharge.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nThis bill adds a new subdivision 9 to 420.10 of the Criminal Procedure\nLaw to require that when a court imposes a fine for a drug or alcohol\nrelated offense under the Vehicle and Traffic Law, the payment of the\nfine will take priority in time over the payment of the surcharge.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe STOP-DWI Program was enacted by the Legislature in 1981 to empower\nlocal efforts to reduce alcohol and other drug-related crashes. The\nSTOP-DWI Law (VTL S 1197) permits each of the States 57 counties and\nthe City of New York to establish a STOP-DWI program which, in turn,\nqualifies the county/NYC for the return of all fines collected for\nalcohol and other drug-related traffic offenses occurring within its\njurisdiction. The Program is funded exclusively by fines that are\nlevied on convicted drunk drivers. Since its inception, the likelihood\nof being involved in a drunk driving crash has been reduced by almost\n70%.\n\nNevertheless, over the years, ancillary legislation has chipped away\nat the viability of this landmark program. In 1983, in order to make\nup for a shortfall in the Crime Victims Compensation Fund, mandatory\nsurcharges were imposed upon convictions for felonies, misdemeanors\nand offenses under the Penal Law and Vehicle & Traffic Law. Over the\nyears, the surcharges have continued to proliferate as a result of\nsubsequent State Budget shortfalls. Today, the three key surcharges\nthat have been conjured up for budget relief add up to $5370 for\nmisdemeanor DWI and $5495 for a felony DWI. Even the traffic\ninfraction of DWAI includes $250 in surcharges. These do not include\nthe $5750 \"Driver Responsibility Assessment\" imposed by DMV, or the\n$525 Crime Victims Fee imposed by the court along with the surcharges.\n\nAs the surcharges have increased, they have had the increasingly\nserious, unintended consequence of affecting both the imposition and\ncollectability of underlying fines imposed for alcohol and other\ndrug-related VTL convictions. With each increase, there has been a\nmarked tendency by the courts to either cut back on the amount of fine\nset for the underlying charge or attempt to collect it through an\ninstallment plan. Moreover, because of the amount of the surcharge and\nthe fact that it is collected first, the defendant is often unable or\ndoes not pay his fine in full (which serves as the primary punishment\nfor this crime). In many of these situations, the courts are either\nunable or unwilling to track the increasing number of cases of\nuncollected fines. As a result, county programs receive less fine\nmoney to fund their successful prevention efforts. If not corrected,\nthis will gradually undermine the financial backbone of the Program.\nTo partly address this problem, this bill would require that when a\nfine and a surcharge are imposed by the court for alcohol and drag\nrelated offenses, the payment of the fine will take priority over\npayment of the surcharge.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThis bill will result in prioritizing funding to the county STOP-DWI\nPrograms.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThe act shall take effect on the first of November after it becomes\nlaw.\n",
